A capture of an idle screen is answered, so a screen recording of a desk
nobody is sitting at is a file with a timeline in it.

ext-image-copy-capture asks its source for a frame only when the session has
damage waiting. A session is created with its whole area damaged, so the FIRST
capture is answered and every one after it waits for somebody to move
something: on a still screen `xdg-desktop-portal-wlr` hands the pipeline one
buffer and then nothing, `pipewiresrc` drops the empty cycles that follow, and
`~/Videos/<name>.mkv` ends with no header in it.

wlroots' own screencopy implementation already calls
wlr_output_update_needs_frame() unconditionally when a client asks to copy, so
this makes the two agree. What it costs is one output commit per capture on a
screen that has not changed, and the client's own rate is what bounds that —
xdpw's max_fps for a recording, a single capture for a thumbnail.

--- a/types/wlr_ext_image_copy_capture_v1.c
+++ b/types/wlr_ext_image_copy_capture_v1.c
@@ -278,10 +278,16 @@
 
 	frame->capturing = true;
 
-	bool schedule_frame = !pixman_region32_empty(&frame->session->damage);
+	// Ask the source for a frame whether or not anything is damaged. A
+	// capture that waits for damage is never answered on a screen nobody is
+	// touching, so a recorder gets the one frame the session's initial
+	// damage owed it and then nothing until somebody moves the pointer --
+	// and a muxer handed one frame writes a file with no timeline in it.
+	// The screencopy implementation beside this one already asks
+	// unconditionally; the rate is the client's capture rate either way.
 	struct wlr_ext_image_capture_source_v1 *source = frame->session->source;
 	if (source->impl->request_frame) {
-		source->impl->request_frame(source, schedule_frame);
+		source->impl->request_frame(source, true);
 	}
 }
 
